            <div class="content-text">
                <div class="table-responsive-wrapper">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Estimated Timeline</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Nature</td><td>Assignment: transfer of ownership | License: permission to use (owner retains)</td></tr><tr><td>Legal Effect</td><td>Assignment: assignee = new owner | License: licensee = user only</td></tr><tr><td>Written Requirement</td><td>Assignment: written + signed (mandatory) | License: written (mandatory)</td></tr><tr><td>Duration Default</td><td>Assignment: 5 years if unspecified | License: as agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement</td><td>Assignment: assignee sues in own name | License: exclusive licensee can sue</td></tr><tr><td>Reversibility</td><td>Assignment: mutual consent/court only | License: revocable per terms</td></tr><tr><td>Best For</td><td>Assignment: permanent IP (M&amp;A, employment) | License: usage (SaaS, syndication)</td></tr>

                    </tbody>
                </table>
                </div>
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:16px;">
                    
                    <p><strong>Paying for work &ne; owning copyright.</strong> S17: author is first owner. S19: assignment must be written + signed. No deed = freelancer owns your core product. Default 5-year / India-only reverts rights unexpectedly. Moral rights (S57) can never be assigned. 2012 Amendment excludes new mediums. Every clause matters &ndash; generic templates create false confidence.</p>

